  "text": "To find the value of the determinant \\(\\begin{vmatrix} \\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \\end{vmatrix}\\) where \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\), we can leverages the trigonometric identities and properties of orthogonality.\n\n### Step 1: Simplify the non-diagonal elements\nNotice that we can rewrite the non-diagonal elements as follows:\n1. \\(\\cos^2 x \\sin^2 x = \\frac{1 - \\cos^2 x}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1 + \\cos^2 x}{2} = \\frac{1 - \\cos^4 x}{4}\\)\n2. \\(\\cos^2 x \\cos^2 x = \\cos^4 x\\)\n3. \\(\\sin^2 x \\sin^2 x = \\sin^4 x\\)\n\nSo the matrix becomes:\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\frac{1 - \\cos^4 \\alpha}{4} & \\sin^4 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\frac{1 - \\cos^4 \\beta}{4} & \\sin^4 \\beta \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\frac{1 - \\cos^4 \\gamma}{4} & \\sin^4 \\gamma\n\\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Simplify the determinant\nThe determinant of the matrix can be simplified using properties of determinants and trigonometric identities. One useful property is that if the rows are linearly dependent, the determinant is zero. Here, we can use the fact that \\(\\cos x + \\sin x\\) and \\(\\cos x - \\sin x\\) are orthogonal for any angle \\(x\\).\n\nThe determinant can be evaluated as:\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha & \\sin^4 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta & \\sin^4 \\beta \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma & \\sin^4 \\gamma\n\\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis determinant can be simplified using the fact that the rows are orthogonal and the columns are scaled versions of each other. The value of this determinant is zero because the rows are linearly dependent.\n\nThus, the value of the determinant is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
